  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "The number of distinct points common to the graphs of \\(x^2+y^2=9\\) and \\(y^2=9\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "infinitely many",
    "B": "four",
    "C": "two",
    "D": "one",
    "E": "none"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A common point must satisfy both equations, so we can replace \\(y^2\\) by \\(9\\) in the first equation. This eliminates \\(y\\) and gives\n\\[\nx^2+9=9,\n\\qquad x^2=0,\n\\qquad x=0.\n\\]\nThe second equation, \\(y^2=9\\), gives \\(y=3\\) or \\(y=-3\\). Therefore the only possible common points are \\((0,3)\\) and \\((0,-3)\\).\n\nBoth points satisfy both original equations, and their different \\(y\\)-coordinates make them distinct. Hence there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) common points, choice C.",
